---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import pkg from '../../package.json';

/*
 * Generated from package.json rather than hand-listed, so the page cannot
 * drift out of date the way a written list would — the failure mode of a
 * colophon is quietly describing a site that no longer exists.
 */
const dependencies = Object.entries(pkg.dependencies ?? {}).sort(([a], [b]) =>
  a.localeCompare(b),
);
---

<Base title="Colophon" description="How this site is built.">
  <h1>Colophon</h1>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      A static site. Every page is HTML on disk before anyone asks for it, and
      the only JavaScript that ships is the filtering on
      <a href="/writing">Writing</a>, <a href="/notes">Notes</a> and
      <a href="/stash">Stash</a> — which is an enhancement, not a requirement.
      With it switched off you get the full unfiltered list rather than a broken
      control.
    </p>

    <h2>Content</h2>

    <p>
      Five collections — tags, writing, projects, notes and stash — defined with
      Astro's Content Layer API and validated with Zod at build time. The
      governing idea is that collections are storage and feeds are views: no
      page reads a collection directly, and draft filtering and date sorting
      exist in exactly one file.
    </p>

    <p>
      Several invariants a schema cannot express are checked at build time
      instead, because Zod validates frontmatter and never sees the body. A post
      with no body and no external link fails the build. So does a note with an
      empty body, a project referencing writing that doesn't exist, a post
      claimed by two series, and a creator whose name is spelled two different
      ways in two stash entries.
    </p>

    <h2>Images</h2>

    <p>
      Cover art goes through Astro's image pipeline for resizing and format
      conversion. Vectors deliberately skip the responsive treatment: a
      <code>srcset</code> of an SVG is several byte-identical files under
      different hashes, which is cost with no benefit. The social card is the
      exception that lives in <code>public/</code> — it is a fixed 1200×630 PNG,
      needs no resizing, and is generated from an SVG source with sharp.
    </p>

    <h2>Built with</h2>

    <dl>
      {
        dependencies.map(([name, version]) => (
          <>
            <dt>{name}</dt>
            <dd>{version}</dd>
          </>
        ))
      }
    </dl>

    <p>
      Requires Node {pkg.engines?.node ?? 'current LTS'}. Type checking is
      TypeScript throughout, including the client-side filtering script.
    </p>

    <h2>Typography and colour</h2>

    <p>
      Not yet decided. The site was built markup-first — semantics, landmarks
      and heading structure before a single colour — on the theory that it is
      easier to style correct markup than to correct styled markup. This section
      will say something once there is something to say.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
